WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.960
Everybody loves style transfer.&nbsp;
This is a task typically done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.680
with neural networks where we have&nbsp;
two images, one for content, and one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:20.400
for style, and the output is the content image&nbsp;
reimagined with this new style. The cool thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.320
is that the style can be a different photo,&nbsp;
a famous painting, or even, wooden patterns.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:32.560
Feast your eyes on these majestic images of&nbsp;
this cat reimagined with wooden parquetry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:38.000
with these previous methods. And now, look at&nbsp;
the result of this new technique, that looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:45.360
way nicer. Everything is in order here, except one&nbsp;
thing. And now, hold on to your papers, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:52.160
this is not style transfer. Not at all. This is&nbsp;
not a synthetic photo made by a neural network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:58.000
this is a reproduction of this cat image&nbsp;
by cutting wood slabs into tiny pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.000
and putting them together carefully. This&nbsp;
is computational parquetry. And here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.320
the key requirement is that if we look from afar,&nbsp;
it looks like the target image, but if we zoom in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:16.080
it gets abundantly clear that the puzzle&nbsp;
pieces here are indeed made of real wood.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:22.240
And that is an excellent intuition for this&nbsp;
work. It is kind of like image stylization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.080
but done in the real world.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:31.280
Now that is extremely challenging. Why is that?&nbsp;
Well, first, there are lots of different kinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:38.000
of wood types. Second, if this piece was not a&nbsp;
physical object but an image, this job would not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:45.200
be that hard because we could add to it, clone it,&nbsp;
and do all kinds of pixel magic to it. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:51.360
these are real, physical pieces of wood,&nbsp;
so we can do exactly none of that. The only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.000
thing we can do is take away from it,&nbsp;
and we have limitations even on that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.960
because we have to design it in a way that&nbsp;
a CNC device should be able to cut these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.480
pieces. And third, you will see that&nbsp;
initially, nothing seems to work well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.080
However, this technique does this with flying&nbsp;
colors, so I wonder, how does this really work?

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:19.920
First, we can take a photo of the wood panels that&nbsp;
we have our disposal, decide how and where to cut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.320
give these instructions to the CNC&nbsp;
machine to perform the cutting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:31.200
and now, we have to assemble them in a way that&nbsp;
it resembles the target image. Well, still, that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:37.040
easier said than done. For instance, imagine&nbsp;
that we have this target image, and we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:44.240
these wood panels. This doesn’t look anything like&nbsp;
that, so how could we possibly approximate it?

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.840
If we try to match the colors of the two, we&nbsp;
get something that is too much in the middle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.640
and the colors don’t resemble any&nbsp;
of the original inputs. Not good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:01.840
Instead, the authors opted to transform both&nbsp;
of them to grayscale, and match not the colors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:08.560
but the intensities of the colors instead. This&nbsp;
seems a little more usable…until we realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.440
that we still don’t know what&nbsp;
pieces to use and where. Look.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.840
Here, on the left, you see how the image&nbsp;
is being reproduced with the wood pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.840
but we have to mind the fact that as&nbsp;
soon as we cut out one piece of wood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:30.160
it is not available anymore, so it has to be&nbsp;
subtracted from our wood panel repository here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:36.560
As our resources are constrained, depending on&nbsp;
what order we put the pieces together, we may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:44.400
get a completely different result. But look. There&nbsp;
is still a problem…the left part of the suit gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:51.600
a lot of detail, while the right part, not so&nbsp;
much. I cannot judge which solution is better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.400
less or more detail, but it needs to be&nbsp;
a little more consistent over the image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.760
Now you see that whatever we do, nothing&nbsp;
seems to work well in the general case.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:06.720
Now, we could get a much better solution&nbsp;
if we would run the algorithm with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:12.160
every possible starting point in the image, and&nbsp;
with every possible ordering of the wood pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:15.760
but that would take longer&nbsp;
than our lifetime to finish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:22.080
so what do we do? Well, the authors have two&nbsp;
really cool heuristics to address this problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:28.000
First, we can start from the middle, that usually&nbsp;
gives us a reasonably good solution, since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.600
object of interest is often in the middle of the&nbsp;
image and the good pieces are still available for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:40.160
it. Or, even better, if that does not work&nbsp;
too well, we can look for salient regions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:46.160
these are the places where there is a lot going&nbsp;
on, and try to fill them in first. As you see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:53.200
both of these tricks seem to work quite well&nbsp;
most of the time. Finally, something that works.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:59.920
And if you have been holding on to your papers,&nbsp;
now squeeze that paper, because this technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:07.120
not only works, but provides us a great deal of&nbsp;
artistic control over the results. Look at that!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.160
And that’s not all, we can even&nbsp;
control the resolution of the output,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:18.320
or, we can create a hand-drawn geometry&nbsp;
ourselves. I love how the authors took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.920
a really challenging problem,&nbsp;
where nothing really worked well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:27.840
and still, they didn’t stop until they&nbsp;
absolutely nailed the solution. Congratulations!

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:22.320
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

